    },
    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_criteria",
      "value": "clinical features such as stiffness/spasms/ hyperlordosis, positive antibodies such as anti-GAD, EMG showing continuous motor-unit activity, response to benzodiazepines",
      "quote": "Stiff person syndrome is associated, in most cases, with high level of serum and cerebrospinal fluid antibodies directed at glutamic-acid decarboxylase (GAD65), an enzyme essential for GABA production. The lack of central inhibitory influence causes overactivity in the c-motoneurone system and subsequent rigidity [2]. As a number of anaesthetic drugs interfere with GABA receptors, stiff person syndrome may have significant implications for the administration of anaesthesia. There is evidence that propofol reduces spinal activity by interaction with GABA (A) receptors [4] and there is a report of postoperative improvement of stiff person syndrome symptoms after administration of propofol [5].",
      "confidence": "medium"
    },
